 would be unworkable in my view  but a sectoral approach will possibly be  unfair on businesses in certain parts of  the economy for instance hospitality  just to say to ours restaurants and  other leisure or entertainment venues  that you you'll be last and  there just really isn't a message that  they can work with so what I'm saying is  let's have a standard sled approach to  this ie permission to open is linked to  businesses ability to operate safe  working standards and social distancing  so that gives everybody a route back to  work but it might mean perhaps that they  have to limit the number of their  employees and work to begin with and I  think that is a better way to approach  the release from lockdown and the other  ideas that have been circulating so far  so just be clear what you're saying is  that for example pubs or restaurants  could open much earlier than most people  are expecting if they can prove that  they can be done in a socially distance  way yeah if you take a standards that  approach you're putting health and  safety at heart of this release from  lock down and that's the way to go in my  in my view and it would be overseen by  the Health and Safety Executive but yes  it would allow businesses in all parts  of the economy to innovate perhaps to  change the way they work limit the  number of employees that they have him  work but at least it would give them a  route back and I'm worried that if we're  going down that the sectoral approach it  could have heavy consequences for a city  like Manchester or say Blackpool in our  town that really depends upon tourism  and hospitality so if the impact of a  sectoral approach could hit certain  places hard so what I'm saying to the  Prime Minister is you know think about a  standards layer approach actually create  a national recovery council because we  all need to have a voice in this debate  but these decisions that you are about  to take are crucial for the economy of  the Northwest of England and we need to  be in the room debating it with you at  the same time now there is risks  attached to this as well aren't they  businesses have got a vested interest in  reopening how would you check that  they're continuing to follow the  guidelines well I would have this  overseen by the Health and Safety  Executive so you know if people are  making complaints that procedures aren't  being followed that there can be an  investigation and people can be required  to close again we saw that when we went  into lockdown a lot of confusion and  actually some breaching of the standards  so we don't want to see that again we  need to put safety at the heart of this  but then  overseen by the health and safety it's  active working with public health  England but beyond the safety Sophie as  well we need to start thinking about  building a better economy building back  better so one of the other ideas I would  put forward today is the ongoing  government support for businesses should  be linked to good employment standards  and particularly payment of the real  living wage you know we have many of us  have been at home while other people  have been out there running the country  in supermarkets and care homes and other  places warehouses and they are the  lowest paid people in society and I  don't feel at all comfortable with that  and I think coming from this we should  say ongoing government support or  businesses should be linked to good  employment and very specifically payment  of the real living wage I believe in  this computer last 20 years we've been  overpaying people at the top of  organizations and under paying people at  the bottom and I think we need a real  national commitment to build back better  and recognize that those minimum wage  heroes who've been out there working for  us all need a much better deal than the  one they've had in recent years now the  briefings that we've some journalist  have been getting about the state of  mind of Boris Johnson is that he appears  to be someone who's big priority is  keeping the infection levels low  avoiding a second peak I was interested  to hear you talking there about you know  some of the industries around Manchester  or Blackpool do you think the government  is right to be so cautious yes I think  the government is right to be very  cautious because you know the worst of  all worlds would be an early release on  managed release that then saw the virus  spreading again in parts of the country  I don't think the government can could  or should take any risks in that  direction so I think the premise that is  right to have cautious instincts as is  the health secretary but let's use this  time to debate properly and agree how we  are going to move from the position that  we're in at the moment I think Kirsten  is right to say the public need a sense  of that because otherwise people are  losing hope and they can't can't see  where where we're going they need to see  that light at the end of the tunnel even  if it is some time away we should be  honest with people  about how we're going to move forward at  the same time do you have any concerns  that if the government does start  spelling out more detail about how it  could ease the lockdown it may mean that  people effectively don't adhere to the  proposals there is a risk of that but I  would say if they answer that question a  bit more fully about when this would  happen then at least people will be able  to say okay well nothing is going to  change for so long so at least we have  to get back down that to observing the  rules they have a sense of you know it  changed at a certain point I can create  more discipline around the whole process  to be honest  Sophie so we do need to start answering  some of these questions now we do need  to get people ready but living in a very  different way for instance I'm planning  to have social distancing on public  transport so your things aren't going to  be the same we need to be honest with  the public about all of that but I think  the time is now to start having those  debates and even if there's a day  sometime in May or June when measures  will be will be eased at least people  will know and they can start to plan for  that and just finally and your health  secretary in 2009 and in 2010 a time  when a pandemic was right at the top at  the government's risk register and yet  it looks as if the advice and their  plans are in place at the time which  remained in place right up until  coronavirus hit didn't involve a  lockdown or widespread social distancing  a 2011 document said it would not be  possible to stop the spread of a virus  have we been unprepared for this kind of  event for quite a long time well you're  right I mean there was a lot of work on  pandemic planning in the government that  I was in and we dealt with one swine flu  and the official inquiry into that said  it was highly satisfactory I think we're  going to see in time and inquiry into  this one say that things couldn't should  have been done better and I've been  quite supportive of some of the way  their health secretaries handled things  but I think his predecessor the former  health secretary has some questions to  answer because I don't believe the  parties that were put in place where  were as robots that they needed to be  but those questions are for another day  and you know it's not for now there now  is to get through there  get a country on a path that it means  the poor and B of all businesses in the  economy a chance to become back let's  come back better let's come back with  higher standards for our workers  recognizing what they've been doing for  us in these recent times okay Andy  Burnham thank you very much for being on  the program and spending out some of  your ideas much appreciate it thank you

